---
// Sampath › 06 — Built to Last: intro, then three label/content rows (Documentation, Agentic
// system, Into code) separated by rules. Same row layout as 04 — Components.
//
// The panels are 864 × 472 canvases (`--u` is one Figma pixel), like the anatomy panel.
// Figma's doc panel is 912 wide (its row has no gap); both pages shift left 24 to stay centred.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Section from './Section.astro';
import docPage from '../../assets/images/sampath/21_06-built-to-last__image-185.png';
import avatarDoc from '../../assets/images/sampath/28_06-built-to-last__image-187.png';
import tokenPanel from '../../assets/images/sampath/22_06-built-to-last__screenshot-2026-09-12-at-22-07-39-2.png';
import jsonExport from '../../assets/images/sampath/29_06-built-to-last__screenshot-2026-10-17-at-19-16-00-1.png';
import widgetbook from '../../assets/images/sampath/30_06-built-to-last__screenshot-2026-10-17-at-19-28-53-1.png';
---

<Section tone="dark" labelledby="built-to-last-title">
  <div class="text">
    <h2 id="built-to-last-title">
      <span class="line">Built for more than</span> <span class="line">the first delivery.</span>
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body-xl lead">
      Once the initial journeys were shipped, the focus shifted to making the system legible to
      everyone who would use it. Not just designers and developers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="rows">
    <!-- Documentation · Written for everyone -->
    <div class="row">
      <div class="row-label">
        <p class="text-label-m eyebrow">Documentation</p>
        <h3 class="text-title-l">Written for everyone</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="row-content">
        <p class="text-body-l body">
          Token descriptions were written as usage instructions, not just names. Component docs
          were structured so PMs, BAs, and leads could read and act on them without a
          walkthrough. And so AI agents could too.
        </p>
        <figure class="figure">
          <div
            class="panel"
            role="img"
            aria-label="Component documentation in Figma: the Color page, listing each background token with its light and dark values and a usage description, and the Avatar component page, with its overview, usage guidance and anatomy."
          >
            <Image class="doc doc-color" src={docPage} alt="" widths={[330, 660]} sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 330px, 38vw" />
            <Image class="doc doc-avatar" src={avatarDoc} alt="" widths={[279, 558]} sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 279px, 32vw" />
          </div>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr />

    <!-- Agentic system · Machine-readable by design -->
    <div class="row">
      <div class="row-label">
        <p class="text-label-m eyebrow">Agentic system</p>
        <h3 class="text-title-l">Machine-readable by design</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="row-content">
        <div class="cols text-body-l body">
          <p>
            If screens are going to be built from this system with AI, a token name alone isn’t
            enough. So every color token carries a description of where to use it, like “Use this
            for main background of the screens” on bg-primary. When the variables are exported,
            that description travels with the token into the code. An AI reading it doesn’t have
            to guess what bg-primary is for, because the file already tells it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The real test came in October. The client needed to see the AI assistant chat
            working, and the team planned static screens. Instead, I gave Claude Code the exported
            tokens and a DESIGN.md describing the system, and within three hours I had a working
            prototype that matched the Figma file.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="split">
          <figure class="split-col">
            <figcaption class="text-body-m split-title">Token with usage description</figcaption>
            <div class="code code-centre">
              <Image
                class="token-shot"
                src={tokenPanel}
                alt="The bg-primary variable in Figma, with the description “Use this for main background of the screens” and its light and dark values."
                widths={[232, 464]}
                sizes="232px"
              />
            </div>
          </figure>
          <figure class="split-col">
            <figcaption class="text-body-m split-title">Same token in JSON export</figcaption>
            <div class="code">
              <!-- Shows the top-left 378 × 313 of the export, as in Figma. -->
              <div class="json-crop">
                <Image
                  class="json-shot"
                  src={jsonExport}
                  alt="The exported JSON: each colour token, such as bg-primary, carries its type, its usage description and its light and dark values."
                  widths={[413, 826]}
                  sizes="413px"
                />
              </div>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr />

    <!-- Into code · A system devs can actually use -->
    <div class="row">
      <div class="row-label">
        <p class="text-label-m eyebrow">Into code</p>
        <h3 class="text-title-l">A system devs can actually use</h3>
      </div>
      <div class="row-content">
        <div class="cols text-body-l body">
          <p>
            Developers started building halfway through the UI phase, and they still had no
            proper design system or token structure on their side. Shipping this release
            mattered, but so did what would come after it. The app will keep growing for years,
            through phases, updates and new features, and the real test starts when millions of
            the bank’s customers begin using it. Every change will need to ship fast without
            breaking quality or consistency.
          </p>
          <p>
            So I decided we needed a system in code, a GitHub repo built with Claude Code from
            the same tokens as Figma. The app is built in Flutter, so every component lives in
            Widgetbook, a tool made for Flutter that fits the team’s existing workflow. Each one
            has a live preview and a code snippet devs can copy. It’s early, with icon sizes,
            avatars and dividers so far, but the next developer won’t have to start from a
            screenshot.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="figure">
          <div
            class="panel"
            role="img"
            aria-label="The Sampath component library in Widgetbook: a component tree on the left, a live avatar preview with its Flutter code snippet in the centre, and its properties on the right."
          >
            <Image class="widgetbook" src={widgetbook} alt="" widths={[776, 1552]} sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 776px, 90vw" />
          </div>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  /* ── Intro ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  h2,
  h3 {
    color: var(--text-sampath-secondary);
  }

  /* Two lines from tablet up, as in Figma; on mobile it wraps naturally. */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .line {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  .lead {
    max-width: 500px;
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  /* ── Rows: 160 label column, 48 gap, content; 56 around the rule ─ */

  .rows {
    margin-top: var(--space-56);
  }

  /* Figma's first row has no gap; 48 as in the second row (confirmed by the user). */
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 160px 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-48);
  }

  hr {
    margin-block: var(--space-56);
    border: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--sampath-card-dark);
  }

  .row-label {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--text-sampath);
  }

  /* Figma gap 20, snapped to 24. */
  .row-content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .body {
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  /* Paragraph pairs side by side. Figma gap 20, snapped to 24. */
  .cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  /* ── Doc panel: 864 × 472 canvas ───────────────────────────────── */

  .figure {
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  /* Figma fill #262629 mapped to sampath-card-dark, radius 10 snapped to rounded.sm (user). */
  .panel {
    --u: calc(100cqw / 864);
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 864 / 472;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--sampath-card-dark);
  }

  /* Both pages are cropped by the panel's bottom edge, as in Figma. */
  .doc {
    position: absolute;
    height: auto;
  }

  .doc-color {
    left: calc(100 * var(--u));
    top: calc(70 * var(--u));
    width: calc(330 * var(--u));
  }

  .doc-avatar {
    left: calc(484 * var(--u));
    top: calc(21 * var(--u));
    width: calc(279 * var(--u));
  }

  /* Widgetbook screenshot, cropped by the panel's bottom edge, as in Figma. */
  .widgetbook {
    position: absolute;
    left: calc(44 * var(--u));
    top: calc(53 * var(--u));
    width: calc(776 * var(--u));
    height: auto;
    border-radius: calc(8 * var(--u));
  }

  /* ── Token / JSON split ────────────────────────────────────────── */

  /* Figma radius 10 snapped to rounded.sm. */
  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 0.5px solid var(--sampath-border-white-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--sampath-panel-translucent);
  }

  /* Figma padding 16/18, snapped to 16. */
  .split-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    min-width: 0;
    padding: var(--space-16);
  }

  .split-col + .split-col {
    border-left: 0.5px solid var(--sampath-border-white-subtle);
  }

  /* Figma sets this in Inter 14; site font at the same size (DESIGN.md › Typography). */
  .split-title {
    color: var(--text-white);
  }

  /* Figma padding 14/12 snapped to 12, radius 12 to rounded.sm (user). */
  .code {
    display: flex;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: var(--space-12);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--sampath-bg-dark);
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .code-centre {
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .token-shot {
    width: 232px;
    height: auto;
  }

  .json-crop {
    flex: none;
    width: 378px;
    height: 313px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .json-shot {
    width: 109.17%;
    max-width: none;
    height: auto;
    margin-left: -1.73%;
  }

  /* ── Tablet: the label sits above its content ──────────────────── */

  @media (max-width: 1199px) {
    .row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-24);
    }
  }

  /* ── Mobile: token panels stack; 32 either side of the rule, as in 04 ── */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    hr {
      margin-block: var(--space-32);
    }

    /* Doc panel zooms in 1.25×, like 03 and 04 (confirmed by the user); centred crop of
       86.4 Figma px off each side. */
    .panel {
      --u: calc(1.25 * 100cqw / 864);
      aspect-ratio: 691.2 / 472;
    }

    .doc,
    .widgetbook {
      margin-left: calc(-86.4 * var(--u));
    }

    .split,
    .cols {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .cols {
      gap: var(--space-16);
    }

    .split-col + .split-col {
      border-top: 0.5px solid var(--sampath-border-white-subtle);
      border-left: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
